Lab: Automating Ceph Node and Pool Management with Ansible
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Configure an Ansible inventory and control node for managing a Ceph cluster
Write an Ansible playbook to add a new OSD node to an existing Ceph cluster
Write an Ansible playbook to create and verify a Ceph storage pool
Apply infrastructure-as-code practices (idempotency, variables, safety checks) to Ceph administration
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Ceph storage architecture (OSDs, MONs, pools)
Familiarity with Linux command line operations and YAML syntax
Basic understanding of Ansible concepts (inventory, playbooks, tasks, modules)
SSH key-based access already configured between the control node and cluster nodes
This lab uses a pre-provisioned lab environment consisting of one Ansible control node and a 3-node Ceph cluster (already deployed and healthy) with one spare, unused disk (/dev/sdb) on each Ceph node reserved for this exercise. No manual Ceph installation is required.

Install Ansible on the control node if it is not already present:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y ansible
ansible --version
Task 1: Configure Ansible and Add a New OSD
In this task you will set up the inventory, then write and run a playbook that adds a new OSD using an existing free disk on a cluster node.

Step 1.1: Create the inventory
mkdir -p ~/ceph-ansible
cd ~/ceph-ansible

cat > inventory.ini << 'EOF'
[ceph-mon]
ceph-node1 ansible_host=10.0.1.10 ansible_user=student

[ceph-osd]
ceph-node1 ansible_host=10.0.1.10 ansible_user=student
ceph-node2 ansible_host=10.0.1.11 ansible_user=student
ceph-node3 ansible_host=10.0.1.12 ansible_user=student

[all:vars]
ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/id_rsa
ansible_ssh_common_args='-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no'
EOF
Deliverable: confirm connectivity to every node.

ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping
Expected result: each host (ceph-node1, ceph-node2, ceph-node3) returns "ping": "pong" with a SUCCESS status.

Step 1.2: Write the OSD addition playbook
Create add-osd.yml. This playbook targets a single OSD node (passed as -l), checks that the target disk exists and is not already part of an OSD, then creates the OSD using ceph-volume. It is safe to re-run: if the disk is already in use, the creation step is skipped.

cat > add-osd.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Add a new OSD to the Ceph cluster
  hosts: ceph-osd
  become: yes
  gather_facts: yes

  vars:
    osd_device: /dev/sdb

  tasks:
    - name: Verify the target device exists
      stat:
        path: "{{ osd_device }}"
      register: device_stat

    - name: Fail if the device is missing
      fail:
        msg: "Device {{ osd_device }} does not exist on {{ inventory_hostname }}"
      when: not device_stat.stat.exists

    - name: Check whether the device already has a filesystem or LVM signature
      command: blkid {{ osd_device }}
      register: blkid_result
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Create the OSD from the raw device
      command: ceph-volume lvm create --data {{ osd_device }}
      register: osd_create_result
      when: blkid_result.rc != 0

    - name: Report skip reason if device was already in use
      debug:
        msg: "Skipping OSD creation on {{ inventory_hostname }}: {{ osd_device }} already has a signature (blkid rc=0)"
      when: blkid_result.rc == 0

    - name: Display OSD creation output
      debug:
        var: osd_create_result.stdout_lines
      when: osd_create_result is defined and osd_create_result.stdout_lines is defined
EOF
Deliverable: run the playbook against one node and verify the OSD joined the cluster.

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini add-osd.yml -l ceph-node2
Then, from the monitor node, confirm the new OSD is listed and the cluster is healthy:

ansible ceph-mon -i inventory.ini -b -m command -a "ceph osd tree"
ansible ceph-mon -i inventory.ini -b -m command -a "ceph health"
Expected result: ceph osd tree shows the new OSD entry with status up and in, and ceph health reports HEALTH_OK (or HEALTH_WARN only if rebalancing is still in progress).

Task 2: Create and Verify a Ceph Storage Pool
In this task you will write a playbook that creates a replicated pool with an idempotent check, then verify the pool exists with the correct settings.

Step 2.1: Write the pool creation playbook
cat > create-pool.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Create a Ceph storage pool
  hosts: ceph-mon
  become: yes
  gather_facts: no

  vars:
    pool_name: lab-rbd-pool
    pool_pg_num: 32
    pool_size: 3
    pool_min_size: 2

  tasks:
    - name: Get current list of pools
      command: ceph osd lspools
      register: current_pools
      changed_when: false

    - name: Create the pool if it does not already exist
      command: ceph osd pool create {{ pool_name }} {{ pool_pg_num }}
      when: pool_name not in current_pools.stdout
      register: pool_create_result

    - name: Set pool replication size
      command: ceph osd pool set {{ pool_name }} size {{ pool_size }}

    - name: Set pool minimum size
      command: ceph osd pool set {{ pool_name }} min_size {{ pool_min_size }}

    - name: Enable the RBD application on the pool
      command: ceph osd pool application enable {{ pool_name }} rbd
      failed_when: false

    - name: Get detailed pool information
      command: ceph osd pool ls detail
      register: pool_details
      changed_when: false

    - name: Display pool details
      debug:
        var: pool_details.stdout_lines
EOF
Step 2.2: Run the playbook and verify the pool
ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini create-pool.yml
Deliverable: confirm the pool exists with the requested settings.

ansible ceph-mon -i inventory.ini -b -m command -a "ceph osd pool get lab-rbd-pool size"
ansible ceph-mon -i inventory.ini -b -m command -a "ceph osd pool get lab-rbd-pool min_size"
Expected result: size: 3 and min_size: 2 are returned, and lab-rbd-pool appears in the output of ceph osd lspools.

Step 2.3: Confirm idempotency
Re-run the playbook a second time without changing any variables:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini create-pool.yml
Expected result: the "Create the pool if it does not already exist" task reports skipped because the pool already exists, while the size, min_size, and application enable tasks report ok (or changed only on the first application-enable call), confirming the playbook is safe to re-run.

Expected Outcomes
A working Ansible inventory and control setup capable of targeting Ceph MON and OSD nodes
A new OSD added to the cluster through an idempotent Ansible playbook, verified via ceph osd tree
A replicated storage pool created, configured, and verified through Ansible, confirmed safe to re-run
Troubleshooting
Issue: ansible-playbook fails with UNREACHABLE! ... Permission denied (publickey) Fix: confirm the SSH key path in inventory.ini matches your actual key and the public key is authorized on the target host:

ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub student@10.0.1.11
Issue: add-osd.yml fails with stderr: 'blkid: /dev/sdb: command not found' or the OSD creation task fails with RuntimeError: Cannot use device, already a member of LVM Fix: the disk already has data or an LVM signature; wipe it first, then re-run the playbook:

ansible ceph-node2 -i inventory.ini -b -m command -a "wipefs -a /dev/sdb"
ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini add-osd.yml -l ceph-node2
Issue: create-pool.yml fails with Error ERANGE: pg_num N is too small or a PG-count health warning after creation Fix: lower pool_pg_num in the playbook to match cluster size (e.g. 16 for a 3-OSD cluster) and re-run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini create-pool.yml
Conclusion
In this lab, you used Ansible to automate two core Ceph administrative tasks: adding a new OSD to an existing cluster and creating a properly configured, idempotent storage pool. You built an inventory targeting MON and OSD node groups, wrote playbooks that check state before acting, and verified every change directly against the cluster using ceph osd tree, ceph health, and ceph osd pool get. These patterns â checking existing state before making changes, using variables for reusable configuration, and verifying results after each run â are the foundation of infrastructure-as-code practice for storage systems. From here, you can extend these playbooks to add MON nodes, manage multiple pools with a loop over a variable list, or integrate them into a larger site-wide playbook that manages the full cluster lifecycle.
